    <!-- PrivacyConsentActivity — shown once, before MainActivity ever
         requests the OS location permission, not folded into the
         existing location_needed screen (which is about the permission
         being denied, not about disclosure before ever asking). Every
         claim here is checkable against what the app's code actually
         does, not boilerplate legal copy: point 1 is why this screen
         exists before the permission dialog at all; point 2/3 describe
         CalibrationManager's real yaw-alignment use of GPS and the real
         "Simulate GNSS blackout" toggle already in the Simulation tab;
         point 4 is true because recording (DevRecorder) defaults off and
         only ever writes to this device's own app-private storage unless
         the user explicitly signs in AND opts in (see SUPABASE.md); point
         5 points at the real OS permission settings, the actual
         mechanism, not a fabricated in-app toggle. -->
    <string name="privacy_title">Privacy &amp; Security</string>
    <string name="privacy_subtitle">Before this app can track your position, here\'s exactly how it uses your location.</string>
    <string name="privacy_point_1_title">Your consent comes first</string>
    <string name="privacy_point_1_body">This app will not request location access until you agree here. Nothing runs behind that request.</string>
    <string name="privacy_point_2_title">GPS sets your starting point</string>
    <string name="privacy_point_2_body">Your phone\'s motion sensors do the actual tracking. GPS is used to fix an initial position and to align that tracking to true heading.</string>
    <string name="privacy_point_3_title">You can watch it run GPS-free</string>
    <string name="privacy_point_3_body">Once tracking has a reference point, the Simulation tab\'s \"Simulate GNSS blackout\" makes the app ignore incoming GPS so you can see motion-sensor-only tracking work on its own — a demo of the dead-reckoning math, not a control over your phone\'s GPS radio.</string>
    <string name="privacy_point_4_title">Nothing leaves your phone by default</string>
    <string name="privacy_point_4_body">Location data stays in this app\'s private storage. It\'s only ever sent anywhere if you separately sign in and opt in to contributing drive data.</string>
    <string name="privacy_point_5_title">Revoke it whenever you want</string>
    <string name="privacy_point_5_body">Location access is a normal Android permission — turn it off anytime in this app\'s system Settings, and the app will ask again next time it needs it.</string>
    <string name="privacy_agree_button">I understand — continue</string>
</resources>
